(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-inner-ear-hearing-loss-vignettes"]={"title":"Inner Ear and Hearing Loss Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 70-year-old man on a known ototoxic agent is being reviewed. Which additional organ should be assessed?","choices":["The pancreas","The adrenal gland","The kidney","The liver"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ototoxic drugs are frequently nephrotoxic and vice versa. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has isolated sensorineural hearing loss with no systemic symptoms. Should an autoimmune screen be sent?","choices":["Yes, but only if over sixty","Never, under any circumstances","No — test only when the picture suggests it","Yes, in every patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct; routine screening is not warranted. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus and vertigo. Syphilis is suspected. Which test should NOT be relied on?","choices":["The Venereal Disease Research Laboratory test","Fluorescent treponemal antibody testing","Audiometry","Otoscopy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is specifically described as not helpful here. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man attends for a routine visit with no hearing complaint. What is appropriate?","choices":["An urgent magnetic resonance scan","Tympanometry only","Screen his hearing","No action unless he complains"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — screening is routine from age sixty-five. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on furosemide, aspirin and platinum-based chemotherapy develops bilateral hearing loss. What should be considered?","choices":["Only the chemotherapy is relevant","All three agents are ototoxic","Only the aspirin is relevant","Only the furosemide is relevant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — each is named as such. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man in the acute phase of vestibular neuronitis is vomiting repeatedly. Which combination is appropriate?","choices":["Immediate surgery","Meclizine and an antiemetic","Antibiotics and an antiemetic","A diuretic and salt restriction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; the role of oral corticosteroids is uncertain. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with tinnitus asks what she can change herself. Which advice fits?","choices":["Stop all exercise","Avoid loud noise and stimulants, and rest adequately","Spend an hour daily in complete silence","Increase caffeine intake"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with daily exercise and having lead levels checked. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl with fullness after a cold has a tympanogram whose peak is shifted well into negative pressure. What does this indicate?","choices":["Middle ear fluid","A normal middle ear","Ossicular fixation","Eustachian tube dysfunction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a type C trace shows negative middle ear pressure. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has reduced hearing on the right. A tuning fork on the forehead is loudest on the left, and on the right air conduction exceeds bone conduction. Which diagnosis best fits?","choices":["Acoustic neuroma","Cerumen impaction","Otosclerosis","Middle ear effusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These findings are sensorineural, and this is a sensorineural cause. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man leaves a concert with muffled hearing, a sensation of fullness and a chirping noise. By the next afternoon his hearing is back to normal. What has happened?","choices":["Sudden sensorineural hearing loss","A temporary threshold shift","A permanent threshold shift","Acoustic trauma with perforation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — but repeated exposures make the loss permanent. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has bilateral gradual hearing loss and says background noise makes it harder to follow conversation. Both drums and canals look normal. What Rinne result is expected?","choices":["Bone conduction greater than air on the right only","No response to either bilaterally","Air conduction greater than bone, bilaterally","Bone conduction greater than air, bilaterally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sensorineural loss leaves the normal Rinne relationship intact. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus, aural fullness and episodic vertigo. Which treatable cause must be excluded because it is indistinguishable?","choices":["Otosclerosis","Presbycusis","Exostosis","Syphilis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — its presentation is often identical, and it is treatable. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 31-year-old surfer has gradual hearing loss and repeated trapping of debris in both canals. Both canals are narrowed symmetrically by hard, smooth swellings of the canal wall. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Exostoses","Cerumen impaction","Canal carcinoma","Cholesteatoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bony growths from repeated cold water exposure. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man standing beside an explosion has immediate hearing loss and a perforated drum. What describes this injury?","choices":["Acoustic trauma","Noise-induced hearing loss","Barotrauma","Presbycusis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a single loud noise causing immediate loss. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has acute vertigo with facial weakness, loss of pain sensation on one side of his face and the opposite side of his body, and a drooping eyelid. What does this require?","choices":["Routine audiometry","Emergency evaluation for stroke","The Epley manoeuvre","Reassurance and meclizine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — crossed sensory findings mean a brainstem problem. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man develops sudden hearing loss and vertigo immediately after lifting a heavy weight, hearing a pop at the moment of onset. What is the likely mechanism?","choices":["Debris in the semicircular canal","Fixation of the stapes","An abnormal opening at the round or oval window","Inflammation of the vestibular nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with sudden one-sided hearing loss asks why she must be seen the same day. What should she be told?","choices":["It always signals a tumour","The other ear will be affected by tomorrow","Treatment works only if started early","Surgery must be done within hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"The daughter of an 80-year-old woman with age-related hearing loss asks how to make conversation easier. What advice is best?","choices":["Shout from across the room","Communicate only in writing","Speak only into her better ear from behind","Face her and speak clearly without shouting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the problem is discrimination, not volume. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 53-year-old woman has episodic vertigo thought to arise from one ear. Which study is the gold standard for a disorder affecting one ear at a time?","choices":["The Weber test","Electronystagmography","Tympanometry","Otoscopy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has recurrent attacks of spinning that last three to four hours, with nausea, a low roaring noise and a feeling of pressure in the right ear. Her hearing fluctuates. Which feature separates this from positional vertigo?","choices":["Hearing is affected","Nausea is present","The onset is sudden","It is one-sided"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — positional vertigo leaves hearing untouched and causes no tinnitus. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with unexplained sensorineural hearing loss has multiple risk factors for sexually transmitted infection. What should be considered?","choices":["Human immunodeficiency virus and syphilis","Cerumen impaction","Noise-induced loss","Barotrauma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both can produce sensorineural loss. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman has severe vertigo for three days. Which two findings would move the diagnosis away from vestibular neuronitis?","choices":["Symptoms lasting several days","Hearing loss or focal neurological signs","Unsteadiness on walking","Sudden onset"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — neither belongs to this condition. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman has bilateral hearing loss that has worsened in steps over two years, with periods of stability between. What does this pattern suggest?","choices":["Noise-induced loss","Otosclerosis","An autoimmune cause","Presbycusis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — deterioration alternating with stabilisation is characteristic. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has a three-year history of gradual hearing loss in the left ear with a normal-looking drum, and says she hears better in noisy rooms. Which tuning fork findings are expected?","choices":["Weber to the right; left air conduction greater than bone","Weber to the right; left bone conduction greater than air","Weber to the left; left air conduction greater than bone","Weber to the left; left bone conduction at least equal to air"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Otosclerosis is a conductive loss. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man says he can hear people speaking but cannot make out the words, and he misses the doorbell. Both ears are affected equally and his canals and drums are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Otosclerosis","Acoustic neuroma","Meniere disease","Presbycusis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bilateral, symmetrical, gradual high-frequency loss. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has reduced hearing. His tympanogram is a flat line with no pressure peak. Which finding does this indicate?","choices":["A normal middle ear","A fixed stapes","Ossicular discontinuity","Fluid in the middle ear"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a type B trace. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has brief spinning attacks whenever she rolls over in bed, each lasting under a minute, with normal hearing. Which test should be performed?","choices":["The Weber test","The Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre","The Epley manoeuvre","Tympanometry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it provokes the classic eye movements. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man speaks noticeably loudly and struggles most in a crowded room. What does this suggest?","choices":["A sensorineural loss","A conductive loss","A functional loss","Wax impaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the loud voice and worsening in noise both fit. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a known acoustic neuroma develops facial numbness and weakness. Which cranial nerves has the tumour involved?","choices":["The fifth and seventh","The third and fourth","The ninth and tenth","The first and second"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with sudden vertigo and hearing loss develops a fever of thirty-nine degrees Celsius. How does this change management?","choices":["Stop all treatment","Arrange a hearing aid","Reassure and discharge","Add antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — antibiotics are given if bacterial features are present. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"}]};
